{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>To strike my enemies I took you, etc.</b> Balak was indignant about the blessing, because even though Balaam said to him, ״הֲיָכוֹל אוּכַל דַּבֵּר״ etc. (Numbers 22:38), he understood that the intention was to the effect that if HaShem does not agree with him to curse, he will not curse — but as for blessing, it is a matter of sit and do nothing, that is, he will not bless. And even though he also said to him, ״הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר יָשִׂים אֱלֹהִים בְּפִי אוֹתוֹ אֲשִׂים״, he thought that this concerns the matter of cursing: that if HaShem puts in his mouth to curse, he will do whatever his soul desires, but if He puts blessing in his mouth, he will restrain himself and not bless — for it is not proper conduct to cause harm to the one who sent for him to bless his enemies, for that is nothing but cursing Balak himself. And this is what he examined closely in the word <b>li</b> ('for me') that he said, <b>מֶה עָשִׂיתָ לִי</b> — for the blessing of Israel is a curse to him." "<b>And behold, you have blessed — blessed.</b> The reason for the doubled expression will be explained by reflecting further on Balak, that he was expressing astonishment at Balaam. And upon Balak himself too one may wonder: that he waited for Balaam until he finished his blessings and did not clap his hands as he did later (Numbers 24:10) to stop him from blessing further — and who would be more anxious about this than he? However, Balak knew that Balaam was cunning, scheming to outsmart and curse Israel, and therefore he was praising and blessing with great exaggeration, so as to say afterward lowliness and disgrace and curses. And you will find that even within the blessing itself he was scheming to say a kind of blessing that contained a curse, as they said in Midrash Yelammedenu (Yalkut Shimoni 761), and this is their language: ״Even though the wicked praised them, he sought to disparage them״ — see there for their words. See, he was scheming even in the blessing itself to curse them. Therefore Balak was waiting for Balaam to hear what he would say in the end, and once he stood revealed, it became clear that what he was blessing Israel was not for an evil purpose but for a good purpose, and this is what he said: ״בֵּרַכְתָּ בָרֵךְ״." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that Balak's vexation was over what he heard from Balaam's mouth — that he expressed a desire to be like them. This would indicate that what he blessed came from his heart and was intended as blessing. And this is what he said: <b>בֵּרַכְתָּ</b> — you made them into a blessing, for you said, ״וּתְהִי אַחֲרִיתִי כָּמוֹהוּ״, and therefore he examined closely and said בָרֵךְ with a full mouth — for others are blessed to be like them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {1 "The verse citation in the text reads (Numbers 24:25); the correct reference for Balak's clapping his hands is Numbers 24:10, rendered accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:13.811978500Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}